# The datastore block is deliberately absent. It names an S3 bucket, and a
# bucket name is the one piece of this file worth not publishing -- the bucket
# is locked down, so the name is an information disclosure rather than an
# access hole, but it still makes a target easier to find. Same reasoning as
# swamp-homelab, which tracks only its .example copy for exactly this reason.
#
# To bootstrap locally, copy this to .swamp.yaml and run:
#   swamp datastore setup extension @swamp/s3-datastore \
#     --namespace "$(scutil --get ComputerName)-unifi" \
#     --config '{"bucket":"<bucket>","region":"<region>","prefix":"swamp"}'
#
# The namespace is prefixed with the HOST name, not a generic "laptop". Each
# machine that runs the drift watchers keeps its own namespace, so two hosts
# checking the same controller never write over each other's history. This host
# is `ufo`; the namespace here is `ufo-unifi`.
#
# Renaming a namespace later is NOT just an S3 prefix copy -- swamp caches the
# namespace under ~/.swamp/repos/<repoId>/<namespace>/ with a sibling
# .datastore-index.json, and it will re-materialise any OTHER namespace it
# still finds in the bucket as a subdirectory of the current one. Set the name
# you want up front.
